Stereotype Threat / Cognitive Load - Adjustment Disorder - V62.89 (Target of perceived adverse discrimination)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Evaluation anticipatory anxiety, Situational working memory reduction, Defensive perfectionism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-043
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of severe chest palpitations, profuse sweating, and cognitive paralysis mimicking an acute cardiac event prior to a major professional evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute onset of resting tachycardia over 120 bpm, severe diaphoresis, and a complete inability to formulate sentences (cognitive paralysis) hours before a high-stakes professional review. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to rapidly exclude a stress-induced cardiomyopathy or acute arrhythmia prior to the availability of routine medical services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My heart is racing so fast it hurts, I'm sweating through my clothes, and my brain has completely blanked—I can't remember anything for my review tomorrow.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a history of severe anticipatory anxiety prior to performing tasks evaluated by the dominant group in their workplace. Over the past 48 hours, facing a high-stakes subjective performance review, the patient engaged in extreme over-preparation and defensive perfectionism utilized as a shield against potential bias. Tonight, this hyper-focus on avoiding behaviors that might confirm negative group stereotypes culminated in a severe autonomic crisis. The patient is experiencing acute somatic symptoms of stress including tachycardia, diaphoresis, and a measurable, profound situational working memory reduction (unable to recall basic presentation facts). The patient reports emotional exhaustion from constantly managing the impressions of others and lingering frustration and self-doubt following previous subjective evaluations.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; unable to safely sleep, prepare, or engage in normal cognitive tasks due to severe panic and working memory failure.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of occupational sabotage (choking) and severe burnout due to the unsustainable cognitive load of managing stereotype threat.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 118 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient is visibly distressed, diaphoretic, pacing rapidly, and surrounded by hundreds of meticulously organized flashcards and notes.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, regular rhythm, strong peripheral pulses. No murmurs or gallops. Respiratory: Tachypneic with shallow breaths. Clear lung fields bilaterally. Neurological: Fully conscious and oriented, but exhibits severe situational working memory reduction; the patient struggles to recall their own phone number or the sequence of events leading to the callout, despite being highly articulate otherwise. Musculoskeletal: Severe tension palpated in the cervical and thoracic paraspinal muscles. The clinical exam highlights evaluation anticipatory anxiety; the patient physically trembles when looking at their preparation materials, driven by defensive perfectionism.
Environmental Observations: The dining table is covered in perfectly aligned, color-coded notes, highlighting the extreme defensive over-preparation.
Medication Reconciliation: No current prescription medications. Denies excessive caffeine or illicit stimulant use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Stereotype Threat / Cognitive Load - Adjustment Disorder - V62.89 (Target of perceived adverse discrimination) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of central, crushing chest pain, normal oxygen saturations, and symptoms being explicitly triggered by the impending professional evaluation rather than physical exertion.

Thyrotoxic Crisis - Ruled out/unlikely despite the tachycardia and diaphoresis, due to the lack of fever, normal thyroid exam, and the episodic, highly specific psychological trigger of the performance review.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, as the severe cognitive paralysis and panic are strictly localized to stereotype threat during high-stakes evaluations by the dominant group, rather than generalized, free-floating worry.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe autonomic hyperarousal, including marked tachycardia (HR 118), elevated blood pressure (148/92), and profound, acute cognitive impairment (working memory failure) driven entirely by the massive cognitive load of stereotype threat. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to conduct a cardiovascular examination, safely exclude a dangerous arrhythmia or ischemic event, and provide immediate pharmacological down-regulation of the nervous system. Delaying care until the morning would have perpetuated the physiological crisis, ensuring complete sleep deprivation and guaranteeing the exact professional failure the patient feared, firmly justifying urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral propranolol 10mg to block the peripheral sympathetic overdrive, slow the heart rate, and halt the physical tremors and diaphoresis. Issued a single-day medical certificate recommending the postponement of the review due to acute medical illness. Advised the usual GP to arrange neuropsychological testing demonstrating situational working memory deficits specifically during primed stereotype-threat conditions.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately manage the situational working memory reduction safely, enforce a strict "cognitive shut-down": manually pack away all preparation materials into a box and place it in another room; removing the visual stimuli stops the brain from attempting to process the overwhelming information, allowing working memory to slowly reset. To combat defensive perfectionism moving forward, implement a "good enough" protocol for minor tasks: intentionally submit one minor, low-stakes assignment per week with exactly one acceptable flaw; surviving this minor imperfection without catastrophic consequence gradually deconditions the perfectionism reflex.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an executive coach specializing in minority stress and stereotype threat management.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for blood pressure reassessment and long-term psychological support planning.

